-- Lists Shale's C functions past a length, longest first, from the
-- splitter the C recipes share (cookbook/c/items/, a Roc package). A
-- function's length is its body's lines of code; its comment lines and
-- blank lines are counted beside it, never in it, so a well-commented
-- function is not taken for a long one.
--
--   shale cookbook:c/list_long_functions.shale             over c/, past its 95th percentile
--   shale cookbook:c/list_long_functions.shale over=60     past 60 lines of code
--   shale cookbook:c/list_long_functions.shale dir=c/host tests=1
--   shale cookbook:c/list_long_functions.shale self_test=1
--
-- The threshold, unless `over=` sets one, is the 95th percentile of the
-- functions' code lines (the length at rank ceil(0.95 n) of n, sorted), so
-- the run lists the longest twentieth, about. The first line is the
-- distribution: how many functions, and the median, 75th, 90th, 95th and
-- 99th percentiles and the longest. Then one line a function past it:
--
--   path:line: long: name: N code lines (C comment, B blank)
--
-- `tests=1` counts each `TEST(` body as a function too; by default a test is
-- left out of both the distribution and the list, since a test's length is
-- its setup's. `main` is a function like any other. A body that shares its
-- head's line (`{ return x; }`) is one line of code. A `#` line in a body
-- is code.
--
-- `self_test=1` runs it over cookbook/c/plants/list_long_functions/ and holds
-- the distribution and the lists to the plants' known lengths, exactly: the
-- default threshold, an `over=` that a function's comments and blank lines
-- would pass but its code does not, and `tests=1`.
--
-- It is written in Roc, the app
-- cookbook/c/list_long_functions/list_long_functions.roc and the modules
-- beside it (Length, the measure and the listing; SelfTest, the plants'
-- check), over the splitter package cookbook/c/items/, on Shale's Roc
-- platform (roc_platform/README.md); this file is its launcher. It hands
-- every value it is given to roc_platform/run.shale in the checkout this
-- cookbook is in (the `cookbook` mount's parent), which builds the Roc there
-- (cached; the first build after a change to C Shale compiles the platform's
-- host, 25 to 50 s) and runs the program rooted here, so `dir=` is this
-- root's path. A value the Roc reads no `Shale.arg!` of stops the run before
-- anything is built. `reads=inputs` builds and runs nothing, and prints the
-- real path of each file the program is built from (run.shale's header). It
-- is built with `opt=speed`, as map_file is: the splitter's masker reads a
-- line a byte at a time, which Roc's dev build does several times slower
-- than the Shale original's regular expressions; a stop in a speed build
-- names the platform's driver, not the Roc's line. Its goldens, the Shale
-- original's output on the plants, are held by
-- cookbook/c/list_long_functions/check_goldens.shale.
-- budget: programs=10 output_bytes=8000000

const SCRIPT = "cookbook/c/list_long_functions/list_long_functions.roc"
const CHECKOUT = dirname(dirname(real("cookbook:README.md")))
const HERE = real(".")

-- `self_test` is read here by name, as the cookbook's full tier finds the
-- recipes it runs `self_test=1` of. `arg_names()` is every value given; the
-- rest are read by a name made as the run goes, so Shale's own check cannot
-- see them: run.shale holds them to the script's reads.
const SELF_TEST = arg("self_test", "")
local values = {"script=" .. SCRIPT, "in=" .. HERE, "opt=speed"}
if SELF_TEST ~= "" then push(values, "self_test=" .. SELF_TEST) end
for name in arg_names() do
  if name ~= "self_test" then push(values, name .. "=" .. arg(name, "")) end
end
local r = run({"shale", "--root", CHECKOUT, CHECKOUT .. "/roc_platform/run.shale", "--args-stdin"}, {stdin = join(values, "\n") .. "\n", timeout_ms = 1800000, whole = 8000000})
print(trim_end(r.out))
if r.code ~= 0 then fail("list_long_functions: the Roc program stopped (exit " .. r.code .. ")") end
